<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amenirdis I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amenirdis I was a Kushite princess, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashta">Kashta</a> and sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabaka">Shabaka</a>, who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gods-wife-of-amun">God's Wife of Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> in the late 8th century BCE. She was adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shepenupet-i">Shepenupet I</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osorkon-iii">Osorkon III</a>, which gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushites</a> a peaceful hold over Thebes. Her funerary chapel stands at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinet-habu">Medinet Habu</a>, and she in turn adopted Shepenupet II.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amenirdis-i.jpg" alt="Amenirdis I" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marsupium · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Amenirdis_-_Egyptian_Museum_Cairo_-_JE_3420.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
